{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Completion state\n\nThis story is complete. It removed duplicate workflow-owned CAS delivery loops by moving the behavior behind Python core entrypoints, while preserving best-effort defaults for existing callers. The implementation is merged in the three child tickets and is present at epic ref `694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d`.\n\n## Delivered architecture\n\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py` is the single owner of tickets-branch delivery mechanics:\n\n- `push_tickets_branch(..., strict=False)` owns push-first CAS retry, fetch-and-merge recovery, the five-cycle budget, and the one conditional terminal push.\n- Opt-in strict delivery raises `PushDeliveryError` with a stable reason, operational detail, and unpushed-commit count.\n- `commit_and_push_tickets_branch(...)` owns the locked stage, commit, and push transaction used by automation.\n- The private `python -m rebar._store.push` adapter translates typed core failures into process stderr and nonzero status; core functions remain reusable and never call `sys.exit`.\n- Workflow adapters select synchronous strict delivery explicitly. Existing library, CLI, MCP, importer, and ticket-store callers retain best-effort defaults.\n\nThe workflows now contain orchestration only:\n\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` invokes `rebar bridge run`; the Python bridge runner performs one locked strict commit-and-push action with the established bot identity and commit subject.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` invokes the Python push adapter once in strict mode.\n- Neither workflow contains its own fetch/merge/retry loop.\n- Strict workflow failure remains visible to GitHub Actions through a nonzero adapter result after the shared core exhausts delivery.\n\nThis is a Python strangler migration, consistent with the projects architecture. YAML remains the workflow declaration layer, while locking, CAS recovery, retry policy, staging, committing, error classification, and diagnostics live in Python. No new shell script or shell-only runtime path was introduced.\n\n## Child ownership and merged evidence\n\n- `953a-d616-52d3-4efe` owns strict push semantics, the corrected CAS retry budget, typed failure reasons, and the initial push-only Python adapter. Merged commit: `a8e0f67c827706525becb9b4d3df8a4da738d44e`.\n- `2bc9-75aa-a08a-478a` owns the locked `commit-and-push` entrypoint, dirty-tree handling, commit identity/message, and reuse of the strict push contract. Merged commit: `1532700efd`.\n- `742d-54f1-3b6f-4864` owns both workflow cutovers and removal of the stale raw-git/CAS-loop rationale. Merged commits: `11a026540b`, `039fe7bbde`, and `511c339a63`.\n\nAll three children are closed and completion-certified by principal `b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126` at ref `694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d`.\n\n## Interface, installation, and legacy continuity\n\nThe change deliberately preserves the established interfaces:\n\n- Python library: the strict flag is keyword-only and defaults false; existing public imports and write calls keep their return/disposition contract.\n- CLI: public commands continue through the same store/library seams; internal automation uses packaged Python entrypoints for strict delivery.\n- MCP server: MCP write tools continue through the same Python facade/store path with best-effort defaults, so protocol behavior does not diverge from the library.\n- Automated callers: bridge workflows opt into strict delivery; import, claim, transition, delete, compact, and event append remain best effort unless explicitly adapted.\n- Dual direction: read behavior and reconciliation input are unchanged; the migration affects outbound tickets-branch delivery only.\n\nInstallation behavior also remains continuous:\n\n- brew and pip installations receive the same packaged Python CLI and core modules.\n- MCP-server installation receives the same library implementation used by CLI and direct Python callers.\n- No workflow depends on a repository-only shell helper, editable-install path, or ambient interpreter behavior.\n- Existing workflows and environments that use legacy command vocabulary, status/exit vocabulary, best-effort delivery, bot identity, or commit subjects continue to work. Strictness is explicit at the two workflow boundaries, not silently imposed globally.\n\nNo persisted ticket schema or event vocabulary changed.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nThe child changes passed Gerrit LLM review and CI before merge. The combined oracle covers:\n\n- successful delivery after deterministic concurrent non-fast-forward races in real temporary git repositories;\n- rejected delivery after the retry budget, with typed core failure and nonzero strict adapter behavior;\n- policy-decline versus transport-failure classification;\n- lock timeout, store-epoch guards, narrow refspec, working-tree preservation, and stash restoration;\n- successful strict commit-and-push with the requested author and subject;\n- failed push leaving the local commit recoverable and reporting the unpushed count;\n- default best-effort behavior for legacy Python/library and MCP/store callers;\n- both workflow entrypoints delegating exactly once with no workflow-local retry loop;\n- focused workflow/core tests plus repository lint, typecheck, and full test gates.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] Python core is the sole owner of CAS push/retry behavior.\n- [x] The locked Python commit-and-push operation stages, commits, and delivers the workflow transaction.\n- [x] The bridge workflow delegates delivery once through the strict Python runner.\n- [x] The canary workflow delegates delivery once through the strict Python push adapter.\n- [x] No workflow-local fetch/merge/retry loop remains.\n- [x] Exhausted strict delivery produces a typed core failure and a nonzero automation result.\n- [x] Existing Python library callers remain best effort by default.\n- [x] Existing CLI callers remain compatible.\n- [x] Existing MCP callers remain compatible.\n- [x] brew, pip, and MCP-server installations all execute the same packaged Python implementation.\n- [x] Legacy vocabulary, environments, bot identity, and commit-subject contracts remain usable.\n- [x] Success, contention, and rejected-push paths are covered by observable tests.\n- [x] Gerrit review and CI are green for every merged child change.\n\n## Rollback\n\nRollback is coordinated: restore the previous workflow delivery blocks before reverting the strict Python adapter/core entrypoints. No data migration is required because no persisted schema changed. The local commits produced before a strict push failure remain recoverable and can be delivered after the remote or policy issue is corrected."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786444749247840001,"uuid":"4f41dc51-8cac-4eda-8399-cda814a604a3"}